AMC.add({"comp": "amc12", "id": "2024A", "label": "2024 A", "problems": [{"q": "<p> What is the value of <span class=\"tex\">101 \\cdot 9,901-99 \\cdot 10,101?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">20</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">21</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">200</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">220</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>Write the difference as</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(100+1) \\cdot(9900+1)-99 \\cdot(10,000+100+1) .</span></p><p>Applying the distributive property gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(990,000+9,900+100+1)-(990,000+9,900+99)=100+1-99=(\\text{A})\\boxed{2} .</span></p><p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">x=100</span>. Then the minuend (the first quantity in the subtraction operation) is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(x+1)\\left(x^{2}-x+1\\right)=x^{3}+1,</span></p><p>and the subtrahend (the quantity being subtracted from the minuend) is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(x-1)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)=x^{3}-1 .</span></p><p>The difference is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(x^{3}+1\\right)-\\left(x^{3}-1\\right)=(\\text{A})\\boxed{2} .</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-1"}, {"q": "<p> A model used to estimate the time it will take to hike to the top of a mountain on a trail is of the form <span class=\"tex\">T=a L+b G</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> are constants, <span class=\"tex\">T</span> is the time in minutes, <span class=\"tex\">L</span> is the length of the trail in miles, and <span class=\"tex\">G</span> is the altitude gain in feet. The model estimates that it will take <span class=\"tex\">69</span> minutes to hike to the top if a trail is <span class=\"tex\">1.5</span> miles long and ascends <span class=\"tex\">800</span> feet, as well as if a trail is <span class=\"tex\">1.2</span> miles long and ascends <span class=\"tex\">1100</span> feet. How many minutes does the model estimate it will take to hike to the top if the trail is <span class=\"tex\">4.2</span> miles long and ascends <span class=\"tex\">4000</span> feet?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">240</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">246</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">252</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">258</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">264</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>The given data from the first two hikes yield the system of equations</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n1.5 a+800 b &amp; =69 \\\\\n1.2 a+1100 b &amp; =69 .\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>To solve this system, first subtract the second equation from the first equation to get <span class=\"tex\">0.3 a-300 b=0</span>, which implies <span class=\"tex\">a=1000 b</span>. Then the first equation becomes <span class=\"tex\">1500 b+800 b=69</span>, from which <span class=\"tex\">b=\\frac{69}{2300}=0.03</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">a=30</span>. Therefore the model is <span class=\"tex\">T=30 L+0.03 G</span>. Substituting the values for the third hike gives <span class=\"tex\">T=30 \\cdot 4.2+0.03 \\cdot 4000=(\\text{B})\\boxed{246}</span> minutes.</p>\n<p>Note: Expressed in words, this commonly used model is \"two miles per hour plus a half-hour for each 1000 feet of altitude gain.\"</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-2"}, {"q": "<p> The number <span class=\"tex\">2024</span> is written as the sum of not necessarily distinct two-digit numbers. What is the least number of two-digit numbers needed to write this sum?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">20</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">21</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">22</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">23</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">24</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>In order to minimize the number of terms in the sum, the greatest two-digit number, 99 , should be used as many times as possible. Because <span class=\"tex\">20 \\cdot 99=1980</span>, the minimum number of terms is greater than 20 . On the other hand, <span class=\"tex\">2024=20 \\cdot 99+44</span>, so the least number of two-digit numbers needed is <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{B})\\boxed{21}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-3"}, {"q": "<p> What is the least value of <span class=\"tex\">n</span> such that <span class=\"tex\">n!</span> is a multiple of <span class=\"tex\">2024?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">11</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">21</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">22</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">23</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">253</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Because the prime factorization of 2024 is <span class=\"tex\">2^{3} \\cdot 11 \\cdot 23</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">n!</span> is a multiple of 2024 if and only if <span class=\"tex\">n \\geq 23</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{D})\\boxed{23}</span> is the least value of <span class=\"tex\">n</span> such that <span class=\"tex\">n!</span> is a multiple of 2024 .</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-4"}, {"q": "<p> A data set containing <span class=\"tex\">20</span> numbers, some of which are <span class=\"tex\">6</span>, has mean <span class=\"tex\">45</span>. When all the <span class=\"tex\">6</span>s are removed, the data set has mean <span class=\"tex\">66</span>. How many <span class=\"tex\">6</span>s were in the original data set?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Let the number of 6 s in the original data set be <span class=\"tex\">k</span>. Numbers in the original data set have sum <span class=\"tex\">20 \\cdot 45=900</span>, and the data set with the 6 s removed has sum <span class=\"tex\">900-6 k</span>. Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{900-6 k}{20-k}=66</span></p><p>Solving this equation for <span class=\"tex\">k</span> yields <span class=\"tex\">k=(\\text{D})\\boxed{7}</span>. For example, the data set could consist of 7 copies of 6 and 13 copies of 66.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-5"}, {"q": "<p> The product of three integers is <span class=\"tex\">60</span>. What is the least possible positive sum of the three integers?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">13</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">60=10 \\cdot(-1) \\cdot(-6)</span>, and the sum of these factors is 3. It remains to show that no positive sum can be less than 3 . Such a sum would have to consist of one positive integer and two negative integers with smaller absolute value. If the positive integer is greater than or equal to 10 , then the sum is greater than or equal to 3 . The possible sets of factors in this case are <span class=\"tex\">\\{10,-6,-1\\},\\{10,-3,-2\\},\\{12,-5,-1\\},\\{15,-4,-1\\},\\{15,-2,-2\\},\\{20,-3,-1\\},\\{30,-2,-1\\}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\{60,-1,-1\\}</span>. None of these sets of factors has a sum less than <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{B})\\boxed{3}</span>.</p>\n<p>The only other possible choices for the positive integer are 5 and 6 , and in neither case is a positive sum possible. Indeed, if the positive integer is 5 , then the only possible set of factors is <span class=\"tex\">\\{5,-3,-4\\}</span>. If the positive integer is 6 , then the only possible set of factors is <span class=\"tex\">\\{6,-5,-2\\}</span>. In both of these cases, the sum is not positive.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-6"}, {"q": "<p> In <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C, \\angle A B C=90^{\\circ}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B A=B C=\\sqrt{2}</span>. Points <span class=\"tex\">P_{1}, P_{2}, \\ldots, P_{2024}</span> lie on hypotenuse <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span> so that <span class=\"tex\">A P_{1}=P_{1} P_{2}=P_{2} P_{3}=\\cdots=P_{2023} P_{2024}=P_{2024} C</span>. What is the length of the vector sum</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\overrightarrow{B P_{1}}+\\overrightarrow{B P_{2}}+\\overrightarrow{B P_{3}}+\\cdots+\\overrightarrow{B P_{2024}} ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">1012</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1012</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2023</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2024</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2025</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>For <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq i \\leq 2024</span>, the vector sum <span class=\"tex\">\\overrightarrow{B P_{i}}+\\overrightarrow{B P_{2025-i}}</span> is the vector pointing from the apex of isosceles right triangle <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span> to the reflection of the apex across the hypotenuse, as seen in the figure below.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2024A/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-109.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Its length is 2 times the height of the triangle, namely <span class=\"tex\">2 \\cdot 1=2</span>. Each pair contributes a vector of length 2 , all pointing in the same direction, to the total sum. With 1012 such pairs, the length of the resultant vector is <span class=\"tex\">2 \\cdot 1012=(\\text{D})\\boxed{2024}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-7"}, {"q": "<p> How many angles <span class=\"tex\">\\theta</span> with <span class=\"tex\">0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2 \\pi</span> satisfy <span class=\"tex\">\\log (\\sin (3 \\theta))+\\log (\\cos (2 \\theta))=0?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">0</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>Suppose <span class=\"tex\">\\theta</span> satisfies the equation. Then <span class=\"tex\">\\log (\\sin (3 \\theta) \\cdot \\cos (2 \\theta))=0</span>. This implies <span class=\"tex\">\\sin (3 \\theta) \\cdot \\cos (2 \\theta)=1</span>, so applying the Product-to-Sum Formula</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sin a \\cdot \\cos b=\\frac{1}{2}(\\sin (a+b)+\\sin (a-b))</span></p><p>gives <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2}(\\sin (5 \\theta)+\\sin \\theta)=1</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">\\sin (5 \\theta)+\\sin \\theta=2</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">\\sin (5 \\theta)=\\sin \\theta=1</span>, and the only possible solution is <span class=\"tex\">\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}</span>.</p>\n<p>However, this solution is not valid because <span class=\"tex\">\\sin (3 \\theta)</span> is equal to <span class=\"tex\">-1</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\log (\\sin (3 \\theta))</span> is not defined.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>As in the first solution, <span class=\"tex\">\\sin (3 \\theta) \\cdot \\cos (2 \\theta)=1</span>. Then either <span class=\"tex\">\\sin (3 \\theta)=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (2 \\theta)=1</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">\\sin (3 \\theta)=-1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\cos (2 \\theta)=-1</span>. However, only the first case is possible because otherwise the logarithms in the equation are not defined. In the first case, <span class=\"tex\">3\\theta \\in \\{\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{5 \\pi}{2}, \\frac{9 \\pi}{2}\\}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2 \\theta \\in\\{0,2 \\pi, 4 \\pi\\}</span>, yielding <span class=\"tex\">\\theta \\in \\{\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\theta \\in\\{0, \\pi, 2 \\pi\\}</span>. The two sets have no common values for <span class=\"tex\">\\theta</span>, so the first case yields no solutions.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-8"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">M</span> be the greatest integer such that both <span class=\"tex\">M+1213</span> and <span class=\"tex\">M+3773</span> are perfect squares. What is the units digit of <span class=\"tex\">M?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>Suppose <span class=\"tex\">M+1213=j^{2}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">M+3773=k^{2}</span> for nonnegative integers <span class=\"tex\">j</span> and <span class=\"tex\">k</span>. Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(k+j)(k-j)=k^{2}-j^{2}=3773-1213=2560=5 \\cdot 2^{9}</span></p><p>Because <span class=\"tex\">k+j</span> and <span class=\"tex\">k-j</span> have the same parity and their product is even, they must both be even, and it follows that one of them is <span class=\"tex\">5 \\cdot 2^{i}</span> and the other is <span class=\"tex\">2^{9-i}</span> for some <span class=\"tex\">i</span> with <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq i \\leq 8</span>. Solving for <span class=\"tex\">k</span> gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">k=\\frac{5 \\cdot 2^{i}+2^{9-i}}{2}</span></p><p>To maximize <span class=\"tex\">M</span> it is sufficient to maximize <span class=\"tex\">k</span>, and this will occur when <span class=\"tex\">i=8</span> and <span class=\"tex\">k=5 \\cdot 2^{7}+1=641</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">M=641^{2}-3773</span>, and its units digit is <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{E})\\boxed{8}</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Because <span class=\"tex\">(n+1)^{2}-n^{2}=2 n+1</span>, successive terms in the sequence of squares, <span class=\"tex\">1,4,9,16, \\ldots</span>, differ by successive odd numbers; and because <span class=\"tex\">(n+2)^{2}-n^{2}=4(n+1)</span>, the terms in this sequence that are two apart differ by successive multiples of 4 . The two squares required in this problem differ by <span class=\"tex\">3773-1213=2560</span>, a multiple of 4 . It follows that the greatest such squares are two apart in the sequence of squares, so <span class=\"tex\">n+1=\\frac{2560}{4}=640</span>. Therefore these squares are <span class=\"tex\">n^{2}=639^{2}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(n+2)^{2}=641^{2}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">M+1213=639^{2}</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">M=639^{2}-1213</span>, and its units digit is <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{E})\\boxed{8}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: Shown below is a table of <span class=\"tex\">5 \\cdot 2^{i}, 2^{9-i}, k, j</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">M</span> for each <span class=\"tex\">i</span> (notation from the first solution). Observe that <span class=\"tex\">M</span> is maximized when <span class=\"tex\">k</span> is maximized.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{array}{c|ccccc}\ni &amp; 5 \\cdot 2^{i} &amp; 2^{9-i} &amp; k &amp; j &amp; M \\\\\n\\hline\n1 &amp; 10 &amp; 256 &amp; 133 &amp; 123 &amp; 13916 \\\\\n2 &amp; 20 &amp; 128 &amp; 74 &amp; 54 &amp; 1703 \\\\\n3 &amp; 40 &amp; 64 &amp; 52 &amp; 12 &amp; -1069 \\\\\n4 &amp; 80 &amp; 32 &amp; 56 &amp; 24 &amp; -637 \\\\\n5 &amp; 160 &amp; 16 &amp; 88 &amp; 72 &amp; 3971 \\\\\n6 &amp; 320 &amp; 8 &amp; 164 &amp; 156 &amp; 23123 \\\\\n7 &amp; 640 &amp; 4 &amp; 322 &amp; 318 &amp; 99911 \\\\\n8 &amp; 1280 &amp; 2 &amp; 641 &amp; 639 &amp; 407108 \\\\\n\\end{array}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-9"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">\\alpha</span> be the radian measure of the smallest angle in a <span class=\"tex\">3-4-5</span> right triangle. Let <span class=\"tex\">\\beta</span> be the radian measure of the smallest angle in a <span class=\"tex\">7-24-25</span> right triangle. In terms of <span class=\"tex\">\\alpha</span>, what is <span class=\"tex\">\\beta?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\alpha}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\alpha-\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}-2 \\alpha</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\pi-4 \\alpha</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">\\alpha</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\beta</span> are the smallest angles in these triangles, <span class=\"tex\">\\sin \\alpha=\\frac{3}{5}, \\cos \\alpha=\\frac{4}{5}</span>, <span class=\"tex\">\\sin \\beta=\\frac{7}{25}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\cos \\beta=\\frac{24}{25}</span>. By a Double Angle Formula,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sin (2 \\alpha)=2 \\sin \\alpha \\cdot \\cos \\alpha=2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{24}{25}=\\cos \\beta=\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\beta\\right)</span></p><p>Because both <span class=\"tex\">2 \\alpha</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\beta</span> are acute, <span class=\"tex\">2 \\alpha=\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\beta</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">\\beta=(\\text{C})\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}-2 \\alpha}</span>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2024A/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-111.jpg\" width=\"700\"/></div>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Using complex numbers in polar form, <span class=\"tex\">4+3 i=5(\\cos \\alpha+i \\sin \\alpha)</span>. Squaring gives <span class=\"tex\">7+24 i=</span> <span class=\"tex\">25(\\cos \\alpha+i \\sin \\alpha)^{2}</span>. Similarly, <span class=\"tex\">24+7 i=25(\\cos \\beta+i \\sin \\beta)</span>. Multiplying these two equations yields</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n(7+24 i)(24+7 i) &amp; =25(\\cos \\alpha+i \\sin \\alpha)^{2} \\cdot 25(\\cos \\beta+i \\sin \\beta) \\\\\n625 i &amp; =625(\\cos (2 \\alpha+\\beta)+i \\sin (2 \\alpha+\\beta)) \\\\\n\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{2}+i \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{2} &amp; =\\cos (2 \\alpha+\\beta)+i \\sin (2 \\alpha+\\beta) .\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Because both <span class=\"tex\">2 \\alpha</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\beta</span> are acute, <span class=\"tex\">2 \\alpha+\\beta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\beta=(\\text{C})\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}-2 \\alpha}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: The angles are <span class=\"tex\">\\alpha \\approx 36.87^{\\circ}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\beta \\approx 16.26^{\\circ}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-10"}, {"q": "<p> There are exactly <span class=\"tex\">K</span> positive integers <span class=\"tex\">b</span> with <span class=\"tex\">5 \\leq b \\leq 2024</span> such that the base-<span class=\"tex\">b</span> integer <span class=\"tex\">2024_{b}</span> is divisible by <span class=\"tex\">16</span> (where <span class=\"tex\">16</span> is in base ten). What is the sum of the digits of <span class=\"tex\">K?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">16</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">17</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">20</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">21</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Notice that <span class=\"tex\">2024_{b}=2 b^{3}+2 b+4=2(b+1)\\left(b^{2}-b+2\\right)</span>, and consider the residue classes of this number modulo 8 . If <span class=\"tex\">b \\equiv 7(\\bmod 8)</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">b+1 \\equiv 0(\\bmod 8)</span>, and if <span class=\"tex\">b \\equiv 3</span> or 6 <span class=\"tex\">(\\bmod 8)</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">b^{2}-b+2 \\equiv 0(\\bmod 8)</span>. In each case <span class=\"tex\">2024_{b}</span> is divisible by 16 .</p>\n<p>In all other cases <span class=\"tex\">2024_{b}</span> is not divisible by 16 . Indeed, if <span class=\"tex\">b \\equiv 0,2</span>, or <span class=\"tex\">4(\\bmod 8)</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">b+1</span> is odd, and <span class=\"tex\">b^{2}-b+2 \\equiv b+2(\\bmod 8)</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">2024_{b}</span> is divisible by no power of 2 greater than <span class=\"tex\">2^{3}</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">b \\equiv 1</span> or <span class=\"tex\">5(\\bmod 8)</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">b+1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b^{2}-b+2</span> are both odd multiples of 2 , so <span class=\"tex\">2024_{b}</span> is divisible by 8 , but not by 16 .</p>\n<p>Because <span class=\"tex\">2024=253 \\cdot 8</span>, there are <span class=\"tex\">253 \\cdot 3=759</span> positive integers <span class=\"tex\">b \\leq 2024</span> that are congruent to 3 , 6 , or 7 modulo 8 . The number 3 must be excluded from this total, because the problem statement requires <span class=\"tex\">b</span> to be at least 5 . Thus <span class=\"tex\">K=759-1=758</span>, and the sum of the digits of <span class=\"tex\">K</span> is <span class=\"tex\">7+5+8=(\\text{D})\\boxed{20}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-11"}, {"q": "<p> The first three terms of a geometric sequence are the integers <span class=\"tex\">a, 720</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">b</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">a&lt;720&lt;b</span>. What is the sum of the digits of the least possible value of <span class=\"tex\">b?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">9</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">16</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">21</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>The prime factorization of 720 is <span class=\"tex\">2^{4} \\cdot 3^{2} \\cdot 5</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">r=\\frac{m}{n}</span> be the common ratio of the geometric sequence, where <span class=\"tex\">m</span> and <span class=\"tex\">n</span> are relatively prime positive integers. If <span class=\"tex\">n</span> had any prime factor greater than 5 , then <span class=\"tex\">b=720 r</span> would not be an integer. Analogously, if <span class=\"tex\">m</span> had any prime factor greater than 5 , then <span class=\"tex\">a=\\frac{720}{r}</span> would not be an integer. It follows that <span class=\"tex\">r=2^{i} \\cdot 3^{j} \\cdot 5^{k}</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">i, j</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">k</span> are (not necessarily positive) integers. Furthermore, <span class=\"tex\">|i| \\leq 4,|j| \\leq 2</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">|k| \\leq 1</span>.</p>\n<p>To minimize the value of <span class=\"tex\">b</span>, it suffices to minimize the value of <span class=\"tex\">r&gt;1</span>. Taking <span class=\"tex\">r=\\frac{16}{15}=2^{4} \\cdot 3^{-1} \\cdot 5^{-1}</span> yields the sequence <span class=\"tex\">675,720,768</span>. To check that no lesser values of <span class=\"tex\">r</span> exist, first observe that <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{17}{16}</span> is not a possible value for <span class=\"tex\">r</span>, so both <span class=\"tex\">n</span> and <span class=\"tex\">m</span> are greater than 17 . This means that <span class=\"tex\">m</span> and <span class=\"tex\">n</span> must borrow at least two prime factors each from 720 , but 720 has only three distinct prime factors, so this is impossible. It follows that the least possible value of <span class=\"tex\">b</span> is 768 , and the requested sum of digits is <span class=\"tex\">7+6+8=(\\text{E})\\boxed{21}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-12"}, {"q": "<p> The graph of <span class=\"tex\">y=e^{x+1}+e^{-x}-2</span> has an axis of symmetry. What is the reflection of the point <span class=\"tex\">\\left(-1, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)</span> over this axis?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\left(-1,-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">(-1,0)</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\left(-1, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\left(0, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\left(3, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">f(x)=e^{x+1}+e^{-x}-2</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">f(x)</span> approaches infinity as <span class=\"tex\">|x|</span> increases without bound, the only possible axis of symmetry is a vertical line. If the axis of symmetry has equation <span class=\"tex\">x=</span> <span class=\"tex\">c</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">f(x)=f(2 c-x)</span> for every real <span class=\"tex\">x</span>, which is equivalent to <span class=\"tex\">e \\cdot e^{x}+e^{-x}=e^{2 c+1} e^{-x}+e^{-2 c} e^{x}</span>. Multiplying through by <span class=\"tex\">e^{x}</span> and simplifying gives <span class=\"tex\">\\left(e-e^{-2 c}\\right) e^{2 x}=e^{2 c+1}-1</span>. Because this equation holds for all <span class=\"tex\">x</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">e-e^{-2 c}=0</span> and <span class=\"tex\">e^{2 c+1}-1=0</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">c=-\\frac{1}{2}</span>. The reflection of <span class=\"tex\">\\left(-1, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)</span> with respect to the vertical line <span class=\"tex\">x=-\\frac{1}{2}</span> is <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{D})\\boxed{\\left(0, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-13"}, {"q": "<p> The numbers, in order, of each row and the numbers, in order, of each column of a <span class=\"tex\">5 \\times 5</span> array of integers form an arithmetic progression of length <span class=\"tex\">5</span>. The numbers in positions <span class=\"tex\">(5,5),(2,4),(4,3)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">(3,1)</span> are <span class=\"tex\">0,48,16</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">12</span>, respectively. What number is in position <span class=\"tex\">(1,2)?</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left[\\begin{array}{ccccc}\n\\cdot &amp; ? &amp; \\cdot &amp; \\cdot &amp; \\cdot \\\\\n\\cdot &amp; \\cdot &amp; \\cdot &amp; 48 &amp; \\cdot \\\\\n12 &amp; \\cdot &amp; \\cdot &amp; \\cdot &amp; \\cdot \\\\\n\\cdot &amp; \\cdot &amp; 16 &amp; \\cdot &amp; \\cdot \\\\\n\\cdot &amp; \\cdot &amp; \\cdot &amp; \\cdot &amp; 0\n\\end{array}\\right]</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">19</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">24</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">29</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">34</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">39</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">a_{i j}</span> be the integer at row <span class=\"tex\">i</span> and column <span class=\"tex\">j</span>. It is given that <span class=\"tex\">a_{55}=0, a_{24}=48</span>, <span class=\"tex\">a_{43}=16</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">a_{31}=12</span>. Suppose <span class=\"tex\">a_{54}=d</span>. Then row 5 is <span class=\"tex\">4 d, 3 d, 2 d, d, 0</span> because it is an arithmetic progression with common difference <span class=\"tex\">-d</span>. The arithmetic progression in column 1 gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a_{41}=\\frac{a_{31}+a_{51}}{2}=\\frac{12+4 d}{2}=6+2 d</span></p><p>The arithmetic progression in column 4 gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a_{44}=\\frac{2 a_{54}+a_{24}}{3}=\\frac{2 d+48}{3}=\\frac{2}{3} d+16</span></p><p>Row 4 gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a_{43}=16=\\frac{2 a_{44}+a_{41}}{3}=\\frac{\\frac{4}{3} d+32+6+2 d}{3}</span></p><p>which implies <span class=\"tex\">48=\\frac{10}{3} d+38</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">d=3</span>. Filling in column 3 with common difference <span class=\"tex\">16-6=10</span> and column 1 with difference <span class=\"tex\">12-12=0</span> produces <span class=\"tex\">a_{13}=46</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a_{11}=12</span>. Finally,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a_{12}=\\frac{a_{13}+a_{11}}{2}=\\frac{46+12}{2}=(\\text{C})\\boxed{29}</span></p><p>The full array looks like this:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left[\\begin{array}{ccccc}\n12 &amp; \\underline{\\mathbf{2 9}} &amp; 46 &amp; 63 &amp; 80 \\\\\n12 &amp; 24 &amp; 36 &amp; \\mathbf{4 8} &amp; 60 \\\\\n\\mathbf{1 2} &amp; 19 &amp; 26 &amp; 33 &amp; 40 \\\\\n12 &amp; 14 &amp; \\mathbf{1 6} &amp; 18 &amp; 20 \\\\\n12 &amp; 9 &amp; 6 &amp; 3 &amp; \\mathbf{0}\n\\end{array}\\right]</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-14"}, {"q": "<p> The roots of <span class=\"tex\">x^{3}+2 x^{2}-x+3</span> are <span class=\"tex\">p, q</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">r</span>. What is the value of</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(p^{2}+4\\right)\\left(q^{2}+4\\right)\\left(r^{2}+4\\right) ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">64</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">75</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">100</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">125</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">144</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>By Vieta's Formulas,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\np+q+r &amp; =-2 \\\\\np q+p r+q r &amp; =-1, \\text { and } \\\\\np q r &amp; =-3\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>The expression to be evaluated is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(p^{2}+4\\right)\\left(q^{2}+4\\right)\\left(r^{2}+4\\right)=p^{2} q^{2} r^{2}+4\\left(p^{2} q^{2}+p^{2} r^{2}+q^{2} r^{2}\\right)+16\\left(p^{2}+q^{2}+r^{2}\\right)+64</span></p><p>The first term is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">p^{2} q^{2} r^{2}=(p q r)^{2}=(-3)^{2}=9</span></p><p>To find the second term, note that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(p q+p r+q r)^{2}=p^{2} q^{2}+p^{2} r^{2}+q^{2} r^{2}+2 \\operatorname{pqr}(p+q+r)</span></p><p>Rearranging and substituting yields</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">p^{2} q^{2}+p^{2} r^{2}+q^{2} r^{2}=(-1)^{2}-2(-3)(-2)=-11</span></p><p>For the third term,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(p+q+r)^{2}=p^{2}+q^{2}+r^{2}+2(p q+p r+q r)</span></p><p>so</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">p^{2}+q^{2}+r^{2}=(-2)^{2}-2(-1)=6</span></p><p>Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(p^{2}+4\\right)\\left(q^{2}+4\\right)\\left(r^{2}+4\\right)=9+4 \\cdot(-11)+16 \\cdot 6+64=125</span></p><p>With <span class=\"tex\">i=\\sqrt{-1}</span>, the given expression can be factored as</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(p^{2}+4\\right)\\left(q^{2}+4\\right)\\left(r^{2}+4\\right) &amp; =(p-2 i)(p+2 i)(q-2 i)(q+2 i)(r-2 i)(r+2 i) \\\\\n&amp; =[(p-2 i)(q-2 i)(r-2 i)] \\cdot[(p+2 i)(q+2 i)(r+2 i)]\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">f(x)</span> be the given polynomial. Then <span class=\"tex\">f(x)=-(p-x)(q-x)(r-x)</span> and</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">-f(2 i)=(p-2 i)(q-2 i)(r-2 i)</span></p><p>which is the first bracketed expression above. Furthermore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">f(-x)=(-x-p)(-x-q)(-x-r)=-(p+x)(q+x)(r+x)</span></p><p>Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">-f(-x)=(p+x)(q+x)(r+x)</span></p><p>It follows that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">-f(-2 i)=(p+2 i)(q+2 i)(r+2 i)</span></p><p>the second bracketed expression above. Hence</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(p^{2}+4\\right)\\left(q^{2}+4\\right)\\left(r^{2}+4\\right)=-f(2 i) \\cdot(-f(-2 i))=f(2 i) \\cdot f(-2 i) .</span></p><p>Because</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">f(2 i)=(2 i)^{3}+2(2 i)^{2}-(2 i)+3=-8 i-8-2 i+3=-10 i-5</span></p><p>and</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">f(-2 i)=(-2 i)^{3}+2(-2 i)^{2}-(-2 i)+3=8 i-8+2 i+3=10 i-5,</span></p><p>the requested product is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">f(2 i) \\cdot f(-2 i)=(-10 i-5)(10 i-5)=(\\text{D})\\boxed{125} .</span></p><p>Note: The roots of the polynomial are approximately <span class=\"tex\">-2.757,0.379+0.972 i</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">0.379-0.972 i</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-15"}, {"q": "<p> A set of <span class=\"tex\">12</span> tokens - <span class=\"tex\">3</span> red, <span class=\"tex\">2</span> white, <span class=\"tex\">1</span> blue, and <span class=\"tex\">6</span> black-is to be distributed at random to <span class=\"tex\">3</span> game players, <span class=\"tex\">4</span> tokens per player. The probability that some player gets all the red tokens, another player gets all the white tokens, and the remaining player gets the blue token can be written as <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{m}{n}</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">m</span> and <span class=\"tex\">n</span> are relatively prime positive integers. What is <span class=\"tex\">m+n?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">387</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">388</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">389</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">390</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">391</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>The situation can be modeled by arranging the 12 tokens in a row, with the first player receiving the first 4 tokens, the second player receiving the next 4 tokens, and the third player receiving the last 4 tokens. There are <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{12!}{3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 6!}</span> such arrangements. The given conditions are satisfied if and only if the red tokens appear among the first 4 positions, the white tokens appear among the next 4 positions, and the blue token appears among the last 4 positions, or some permutation of the groups of 4. There are <span class=\"tex\">\\binom{4}{3} \\cdot\\binom{4}{2} \\cdot\\binom{4}{1} \\cdot 3</span> ! ways for this to happen. The required probability is therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 6!}{12!}=\\frac{4}{385}</span></p><p>and the requested sum is <span class=\"tex\">4+385=(\\text{C})\\boxed{389}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-16"}, {"q": "<p> Integers <span class=\"tex\">a, b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> satisfy <span class=\"tex\">a b+c=100, b c+a=87</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c a+b=60</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">a b+b c+c a?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">212</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">247</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">258</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">276</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">284</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Notice that the difference between <span class=\"tex\">100</span> and <span class=\"tex\">87</span> is <span class=\"tex\">13</span>, a prime number. This fact will help to simplify the problem. Subtract the second equation from the first to get</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n13 &amp; =(a b+c)-(b c+a) \\\\\n&amp; =a b-b c-a+c \\\\\n&amp; =b(a-c)-(a-c) \\\\\n&amp; =(b-1)(a-c)\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Thus <span class=\"tex\">b-1= \\pm 1</span> or <span class=\"tex\">b-1= \\pm 13</span>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If <span class=\"tex\">b-1=-1</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">b=0</span>, implying <span class=\"tex\">c=100, a=87</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c a=60</span>, which is impossible.</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"tex\">b-1=1</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">b=2</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a-c=13</span>, implying <span class=\"tex\">c a=58=2 \\cdot 29</span>, which cannot be true if <span class=\"tex\">a-c=13</span>.</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"tex\">b-1=13</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">b=14</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a-c=1</span>, implying <span class=\"tex\">c a=46=2 \\cdot 23</span>, which cannot be true if <span class=\"tex\">a-c=1</span>.</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"tex\">b-1=-13</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">b=-12</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a-c=-1</span>, implying <span class=\"tex\">c a=72</span>, which is satisfied when <span class=\"tex\">a=-9</span> and <span class=\"tex\">c=-8</span>. In fact, <span class=\"tex\">a=-9, b=-12</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c=-8</span> satisfies all three equations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The requested value is <span class=\"tex\">a b+b c+c a=(-9)(-12)+(-12)(-8)+(-8)(-9)=108+96+72=(\\text{D})\\boxed{276}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: There are also four noninteger solutions. When written in the form <span class=\"tex\">(a, b, c)</span>, these solutions are approximately <span class=\"tex\">(0.594,0.869,99.484),(1.715,57.455,1.484),(7.477,12.525,6.349)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">(86.214, 1.152, 0.683)</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-17"}, {"q": "<p> On top of a rectangular card with sides of length <span class=\"tex\">1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2+\\sqrt{3}</span>, an identical card is placed so that two of their diagonals line up, as shown ( <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span>, in this case).</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2024A/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-115.jpg\" width=\"400\"/></div>\n<p>Continue the process, adding a third card to the second, and so on, lining up successive diagonals after rotating clockwise. In total, how many cards must be used until a vertex of a new card lands exactly on the vertex labeled <span class=\"tex\">B</span> in the figure?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12</span>", "No new vertex will land on <span class=\"tex\">B</span>."], "a": "A", "s": "<p>Note that the common diagonal is a diameter of the circle that will ultimately circumscribe the collection of rectangles. Each rectangle is composed of four chords of the circle, and the set of outermost chords will form a regular <span class=\"tex\">n</span>-gon if a vertex lands on <span class=\"tex\">B</span>. Because each new card contributes two sides of the polygon, the problem is asking for <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{n}{2}</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">O</span> be the center of the circle.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2024A/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-116.jpg\" width=\"400\"/></div>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">\\theta=\\angle A C B</span>. Then the measure of minor arc \\wideparen{A B} is <span class=\"tex\">2 \\theta</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">n=\\frac{360^{\\circ}}{2 \\theta}</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">A B=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B C=2+\\sqrt{3}</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\tan \\theta=\\frac{1}{2+\\sqrt{3}}=2-\\sqrt{3} .</span></p><p>Each card is rotated through an angle <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A O B=2 \\theta</span> compared to the previous card. A Double Angle Formula gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\tan 2 \\theta=\\frac{2 \\tan \\theta}{1-\\tan ^{2} \\theta}=\\frac{2(2-\\sqrt{3})}{1-(2-\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} .</span></p><p>This value is recognizable as <span class=\"tex\">\\tan 30^{\\circ}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">2 \\theta=30^{\\circ}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">n=\\frac{360}{30}=12</span>. The polygon is a dodecagon. It will take just <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{n}{2}=(\\text{A})\\boxed{6}</span> cards to complete the dodecagon and have a new vertex land on vertex <span class=\"tex\">B</span>, as illustrated below.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2024A/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-116_1_.jpg\" width=\"400\"/></div>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-18"}, {"q": "<p> Cyclic quadrilateral <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> has lengths <span class=\"tex\">B C=C D=3</span> and <span class=\"tex\">D A=5</span> with <span class=\"tex\">\\angle C D A=120^{\\circ}</span>. What is the length of the shorter diagonal of <span class=\"tex\">A B C D?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{31}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{33}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{39}{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{41}{7}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Place the figure in the coordinate plane with <span class=\"tex\">D=(0,0)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">A=(5,0)</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">\\angle C D A=120^{\\circ}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">C D=3</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">C=\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}, \\frac{3}{2} \\sqrt{3}\\right)</span>. The perpendicular bisectors of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A D}</span> intersect at the center of the circumscribing circle. The midpoint of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}, \\frac{3}{4} \\sqrt{3}\\right)</span>. The line through <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span> has slope <span class=\"tex\">-\\sqrt{3}</span>, so a line perpendicular to that has slope <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}</span>. The perpendicular bisector of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span> therefore has equation</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">y-\\frac{3}{4} \\sqrt{3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\left(x+\\frac{3}{4}\\right)</span></p><p>The perpendicular bisector of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A D}</span> has equation <span class=\"tex\">x=\\frac{5}{2}</span>. Solving this system of equations locates the center of the circumscribing circle at <span class=\"tex\">O\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, \\frac{11}{6} \\sqrt{3}\\right)</span>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2024A/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-117.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>By the Distance Formula, the radius of the circle is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">r=O D=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\frac{11}{6} \\sqrt{3}\\right)^{2}}=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{3} .</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">\\theta</span> be the measure of <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B C O</span>. By the Law of Cosines applied to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B C O</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{49}{3}=B O^{2}=B C^{2}+C O^{2}-2 B C \\cdot C O \\cos \\theta=9+\\frac{49}{3}-2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\cos \\theta</span></p><p>which gives <span class=\"tex\">\\cos \\theta=\\frac{3}{14} \\sqrt{3}</span>. A Double Angle Formula then gives <span class=\"tex\">\\cos 2 \\theta=2 \\cdot \\frac{27}{196}-1=-\\frac{71}{98}</span>. Triangles <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B O C</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle C O D</span> are congruent isosceles triangles, so <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B C D</span> has measure <span class=\"tex\">2 \\theta</span>. By the Law of Cosines applied to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B C D</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">B D^{2}=B C^{2}+C D^{2}-2 B C \\cdot C D \\cos 2 \\theta=9+9-2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot\\left(-\\frac{71}{98}\\right)=\\frac{1521}{49}=\\left(\\frac{3 \\cdot 13}{7}\\right)^{2},</span></p><p>so <span class=\"tex\">B D=\\frac{39}{7}</span>. The Law of Cosines applied to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A D C</span> gives <span class=\"tex\">A C=7</span>, so the shorter diagonal has length <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{D})\\boxed{\\frac{39}{7}}</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>The Law of Cosines applied to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A D C</span> gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">C A^{2}=3^{2}+5^{2}-2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot \\cos 120^{\\circ} .</span></p><p>Because <span class=\"tex\">\\cos 120^{\\circ}=-\\frac{1}{2}</span>, diagonal <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span> has length <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{9+25+15}=7</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> is cyclic, <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A B C</span> is supplementary to <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A D C</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A B C=60^{\\circ}</span>. The Law of Cosines applied to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span> gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">7^{2}=3^{2}+B A^{2}-2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot B A \\cdot \\cos 60^{\\circ}</span></p><p>Simplifying yields <span class=\"tex\">B A^{2}-3 \\cdot B A-40=0</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">B A=8</span>. By Ptolemy's Theorem</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">C A \\cdot B D=B A \\cdot C D+B C \\cdot A D</span></p><p>Substituting gives <span class=\"tex\">7 \\cdot B D=8 \\cdot 3+3 \\cdot 5</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">B D=(\\text{D})\\boxed{\\frac{39}{7}}</span>, which is less than the length of diagonal <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-19"}, {"q": "<p> Points <span class=\"tex\">P</span> and <span class=\"tex\">Q</span> are chosen uniformly and independently at random on sides <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A B}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A C}</span>, respectively, of equilateral triangle <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span>. Which of the following intervals contains the probability that the area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A P Q</span> is less than half the area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\left[\\dfrac{3}{8}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right]</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{2}{3}\\right]</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}, \\dfrac{3}{4}\\right]</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}, \\dfrac{7}{8}\\right]</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\dfrac{7}{8}, 1\\right]</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Without loss of generality let <span class=\"tex\">A B=A C=B C=1</span>; then the area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{3}</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">x=A P</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y=A Q</span>. Then the area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A P Q</span> is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{1}{2} x y \\cdot \\sin 60^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{4} \\sqrt{3} \\cdot x y</span></p><p>The probability that the area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A P Q</span> is less than half the area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A B C</span> is therefore the probability that <span class=\"tex\">x y&lt;\\frac{1}{2}</span>. Graph the curve <span class=\"tex\">x y=\\frac{1}{2}</span> in the unit square whose lower left corner is at the origin, as shown. Note that the curve passes through the points <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, 1\\right)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\left(1, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)</span> and is concave up on the interval <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2}&lt;x&lt;1</span>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2024A/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-118.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>The probability that <span class=\"tex\">x y&gt;\\frac{1}{2}</span> is the area of the upper right \"fat triangular\" region with curved longest side, which is less than <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{4}</span> but greater than <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{8}</span>. Therefore the probability that <span class=\"tex\">x y&lt;\\frac{1}{2}</span> lies between <span class=\"tex\">1-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{4}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">1-\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{7}{8}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: The required area can be calculated to be <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\ln 2 \\approx 0.85</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-20"}, {"q": "<p> Suppose that <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}=2</span> and the sequence <span class=\"tex\">\\left(a_{n}\\right)</span> satisfies the recurrence relation</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{a_{n}-1}{n-1}=\\frac{a_{n-1}+1}{(n-1)+1}</span></p><p>for all <span class=\"tex\">n \\geq 2</span>. What is the greatest integer less than or equal to</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sum_{n=1}^{100} a_{n}^{2} ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">338,550</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">338,551</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">338,552</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">338,553</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">338,554</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>Computing the first few terms of this sequence gives <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}=2, a_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}, a_{3}=\\frac{10}{3}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">a_{4}=\\frac{17}{4}</span>, so it appears that <span class=\"tex\">a_{n}=n+\\frac{1}{n}</span>. Indeed, this is correct, because the recurrence relation is satisfied:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{a_{n-1}+1}{(n-1)+1}=\\frac{n-1+\\frac{1}{n-1}+1}{n}=1+\\frac{1}{n(n-1)}=\\frac{n-1}{n-1}+\\frac{\\frac{1}{n}}{n-1}=\\frac{a_{n}-1}{n-1} .</span></p><p>Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{n=1}^{100} a_{n}^{2} &amp; =\\sum_{n=1}^{100}\\left(n^{2}+2+\\frac{1}{n^{2}}\\right) \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{100 \\cdot 101 \\cdot 201}{6}+200+r \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{2030100+1200}{6}+r \\\\\n&amp; =338,550+r,\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>where <span class=\"tex\">r=\\sum_{n=1}^{100} \\frac{1}{n^{2}}</span>. But by a telescoping sum argument,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1&lt;r&lt;1+\\sum_{n=2}^{100} \\frac{1}{n(n-1)}=2-\\frac{1}{100}&lt;2</span></p><p>Thus <span class=\"tex\">\\sum_{n=1}^{100} a_{n}^{2}</span> is between 338,551 and 338,552 , and the requested greatest integer is <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{B})\\boxed{338,551}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: One could also estimate <span class=\"tex\">\\sum_{n=1}^{100} \\frac{1}{n^{2}}</span> by using the fact that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{n^{2}}=\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{6} \\approx 1.645</span></p><p>as proved by Euler in the 18th century.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-21"}, {"q": "<p> The figure below shows a dotted grid <span class=\"tex\">8</span> cells wide and <span class=\"tex\">3</span> cells tall consisting of <span class=\"tex\">1^{\\prime \\prime} \\times 1^{\\prime \\prime}</span> squares. Carl places <span class=\"tex\">1</span>-inch toothpicks along some of the sides of the squares to create a closed loop that does not intersect itself. The numbers in the cells indicate the number of sides of that square that are to be covered by toothpicks, and any number of toothpicks are allowed if no number is written. In how many ways can Carl place the toothpicks?</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2024A/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-120_1_.jpg\" width=\"700\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">130</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">140</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">146</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">162</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">196</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>There are two possibilities for the loop if it does not cross the middle row of <span class=\"tex\">1 \\mathrm{~s}:</span> an <span class=\"tex\">8 \\times 1</span> rectangle around the top row of cells or an <span class=\"tex\">8 \\times 1</span> rectangle around the bottom row of cells. Otherwise, wherever the loop crosses the middle row, it must proceed straight in both directions after the middle row. This divides the dotted grid into two connected components, and both ends of the loop must enter the same connected component. It follows that the loop must cross one of the leftmost two columns and one of the rightmost two columns for a total of four configurations.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2024A/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-120_2_.jpg\" width=\"700\"/></div>\n<p>In each case there are two ends of the loop that must connect-one across the top and one across the bottom-along with some number of 1 s in the middle of the grid. Suppose there are <span class=\"tex\">n</span> such 1 s . For each 1 , the loop can cover either the top edge or the bottom edge. These choices are independent of each other, so the number of ways to connect both ends of the loop is <span class=\"tex\">2^{n}</span>. The figure below demonstrates one possibility when <span class=\"tex\">n=6</span>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2024A/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-120.jpg\" width=\"700\"/></div>\n<p>Of the four possibilities for what happens at the ends, one gives <span class=\"tex\">n=6</span>, two give <span class=\"tex\">n=5</span>, and one gives <span class=\"tex\">n=4</span>. The total number of solutions is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">2^{6}+2 \\cdot 2^{5}+2^{4}+2=64+64+16+2=(\\text{C})\\boxed{146}</span></p><p>Note: The rules for laying matchsticks mirror those found in the logic puzzle genre Slitherlink, which originated from Japan in the early 1990s.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-22"}, {"q": "<p> What is the value of</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\tan ^{2} \\frac{\\pi}{16} \\cdot \\tan ^{2} \\frac{3 \\pi}{16}+\\tan ^{2} \\frac{\\pi}{16} \\cdot \\tan ^{2} \\frac{5 \\pi}{16}+\\tan ^{2} \\frac{3 \\pi}{16} \\cdot \\tan ^{2} \\frac{7 \\pi}{16}+\\tan ^{2} \\frac{5 \\pi}{16} \\cdot \\tan ^{2} \\frac{7 \\pi}{16} ?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">28</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">68</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">70</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">72</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">84</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{16}</span>, and let <span class=\"tex\">c=\\cos \\theta, s=\\sin \\theta</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">t=\\tan \\theta</span>. Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">e^{8 i \\theta}=e^{\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot i}=(c+i s)^{8}=0+1 i</span></p><p>Expanding the binomial and taking the real part gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">0=1 c^{8}-28 c^{6} s^{2}+70 c^{4} s^{4}-28 c^{2} s^{6}+1 s^{8}</span></p><p>Divide by <span class=\"tex\">c^{8}</span> to get</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1-28 t^{2}+70 t^{4}-28 t^{6}+1 t^{8}=0</span></p><p>and let <span class=\"tex\">p(t)=1-28 t^{2}+70 t^{4}-28 t^{6}+1 t^{8}</span>. In other words, <span class=\"tex\">t=\\tan \\theta</span> and <span class=\"tex\">-t=-\\tan \\theta</span> are roots of the polynomial</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">p(x)=1-28 x^{2}+70 x^{4}-28 x^{6}+1 x^{8}</span></p><p>The same argument with <span class=\"tex\">\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{16}</span> replaced by <span class=\"tex\">\\theta_{k}=\\frac{k \\pi}{16}</span> shows that <span class=\"tex\">t_{k}=\\tan \\theta_{k}</span> is also a root of <span class=\"tex\">p(x)</span> for <span class=\"tex\">k \\in\\{1,3,5,7\\}</span>. This holds because <span class=\"tex\">\\left(c_{k}+i s_{k}\\right)^{8}=0 \\pm 1 i</span> when <span class=\"tex\">c_{k}=\\cos (k \\theta)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">s_{k}=\\sin (k \\theta)</span>. In each case <span class=\"tex\">8 k \\equiv 8(\\bmod 16)</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">x=\\tan (k \\theta)</span> is a root of <span class=\"tex\">p(x)</span> whenever</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">x^{2} \\in\\left\\{\\tan ^{2} \\theta, \\tan ^{2}(3 \\theta), \\tan ^{2}(5 \\theta), \\tan ^{2}(7 \\theta)\\right\\}</span></p><p>This accounts for all 8 roots, so it must be that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">p(x)=\\left(x^{2}-\\tan ^{2} \\theta\\right)\\left(x^{2}-\\tan ^{2}(3 \\theta)\\right)\\left(x^{2}-\\tan ^{2}(5 \\theta)\\right)\\left(x^{2}-\\tan ^{2}(7 \\theta)\\right)</span></p><p>Examining the <span class=\"tex\">x^{4}</span> term in the two expressions for <span class=\"tex\">p(x)</span> reveals that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan ^{2} \\theta \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(3 \\theta)+\\tan ^{2} \\theta \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(5 \\theta) &amp; +\\tan ^{2} \\theta \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(7 \\theta)+\\tan ^{2}(3 \\theta) \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(5 \\theta) \\\\\n&amp; +\\tan ^{2}(3 \\theta) \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(7 \\theta)+\\tan ^{2}(5 \\theta) \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(7 \\theta)=70\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>The identity <span class=\"tex\">\\tan \\theta \\cdot \\tan \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\theta\\right)=\\tan \\theta \\cdot \\cot \\theta=1</span> implies that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\tan ^{2} \\theta \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(7 \\theta)=\\tan ^{2}(3 \\theta) \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(5 \\theta)=1</span></p><p>Subtracting those two terms from the previous expression gives</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\tan ^{2} \\theta \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(3 \\theta)+\\tan ^{2} \\theta \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(5 \\theta)+\\tan ^{2}(3 \\theta) \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(7 \\theta)+\\tan ^{2}(5 \\theta) \\cdot \\tan ^{2}(7 \\theta)=(\\text{B})\\boxed{68}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-23"}, {"q": "<p> A disphenoid is a tetrahedron whose triangular faces are congruent to one another. What is the least total surface area of a disphenoid whose faces are scalene triangles with integer side lengths?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3 \\sqrt{15}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15 \\sqrt{7}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">24 \\sqrt{6}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>First note that adjacent faces must be rotations of one another to create a disphenoid (see the figure below). If they were instead mirrored, then the other two faces would be isosceles (and be neither scalene nor congruent to the others, as required).</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc12/2024A/2025_01_28_7f3e765c72fad8d52df5g-122.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>Next, to determine what triangles could form a disphenoid, let the positive integer side lengths be <span class=\"tex\">a</span>, <span class=\"tex\">b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c</span>, with <span class=\"tex\">a&lt;b&lt;c</span>. Consider two faces of the tetrahedron that have their sides of length <span class=\"tex\">c</span> in common. That common edge can be thought of as a \"hinge\" between two faces. If those two faces were laid flat, they would form a parallelogram with one diagonal of length <span class=\"tex\">c</span>. When folded over the hinge, the other diagonal of the parallelogram becomes two sides of a triangle whose third side is the last edge of the tetrahedron. (Note that this last edge must have length <span class=\"tex\">c</span> to create the disphenoid.) If the sides of length <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> formed a right angle, then the parallelogram would be a rectangle, and the other diagonal would also have length <span class=\"tex\">c</span>. A tetrahedron formed by folding the triangles over the hinge would have its last edge shorter than <span class=\"tex\">c</span>, making it not a disphenoid. If instead the sides of length <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> formed an obtuse angle, the other diagonal would be shorter than <span class=\"tex\">c</span>, and folding would make the last edge shorter yet (and again not a disphenoid). Thus the faces must be acute to form a disphenoid.</p>\n<p>To minimize the surface area, <span class=\"tex\">a, b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> must be the least distinct integers that form an acute triangle. Note that 2, 3, and 4 form an obtuse triangle (because <span class=\"tex\">2^{2}+3^{2}&lt;4^{2}</span> ); and 3, 4, and 5 form a right triangle. However, <span class=\"tex\">4^{2}+5^{2}=41&gt;36=6^{2}</span>, so 4,5 , and 6 form an acute triangle with the least area. The area of one face can be computed using Heron's Formula. The triangle's semiperimeter is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{4+5+6}{2}=\\frac{15}{2}</span>, and the disphenoid's total surface area is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n4 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{2}\\left(\\frac{15}{2}-4\\right)\\left(\\frac{15}{2}-5\\right)\\left(\\frac{15}{2}-6\\right)} &amp; =4 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16} \\cdot 15(15-8)(15-10)(15-12)} \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{4}{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 3}=15 \\sqrt{7}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Another way to find the area of a face is to use the formula <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2} a b \\sin \\gamma</span>. By the Law of Cosines,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\cos \\gamma=\\frac{a^{2}+b^{2}-c^{2}}{2 a b}=\\frac{4^{2}+5^{2}-6^{2}}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5}=\\frac{1}{8} .</span></p><p>Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\sin \\gamma=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{64}}=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{8}</span></p><p>Therefore the area of one face is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{8}=\\frac{15 \\sqrt{7}}{4}</span></p><p>and the total surface area is <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{D})\\boxed{15\\sqrt{7}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-24"}, {"q": "<p> A graph is symmetric about a line if the graph remains unchanged after reflection in that line. For how many quadruples of integers <span class=\"tex\">(a, b, c, d)</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">|a|,|b|,|c|,|d| \\leq 5</span> and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> and <span class=\"tex\">d</span> are not both <span class=\"tex\">0</span>, is the graph of</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">y=\\frac{a x+b}{c x+d}</span></p><p>symmetric about the line <span class=\"tex\">y=x?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">1282</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1292</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1310</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1320</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1330</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>There are two cases, depending on whether the denominator of the fraction is constant.</p>\n<p>CASE 1. Suppose that <span class=\"tex\">c=0</span>. Then the graph of <span class=\"tex\">y=\\frac{a x+b}{d}</span> is a line. It is symmetric with respect to the line <span class=\"tex\">y=x</span> if the slope of the line is 1 and the line passes through the origin, or the slope is -1 . A line with slope 1 that passes through the origin occurs if <span class=\"tex\">a=d \\neq 0</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b=0</span>. There are 10 possible values of <span class=\"tex\">a</span>. The slope is -1 if <span class=\"tex\">d=-a \\neq 0</span>. There are 10 possible values of <span class=\"tex\">a</span>, and 11 possible values of <span class=\"tex\">b</span>, which gives 110 possibilities. This gives a total of 120 possibilities in Case 1 .</p>\n<p>CASE 2. Suppose that <span class=\"tex\">c \\neq 0</span>. The quantity <span class=\"tex\">y=\\frac{a x+b}{c x+d}</span> tends to <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{a}{c}</span> as <span class=\"tex\">x \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty</span>. This is a horizontal asymptote. The degree of the polynomial in the numerator can be reduced by subtracting this asymptotic value:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">y-\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{a x+b}{c x+d}-\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{a c x+b c-a(c x+d)}{c(c x+d)}=\\frac{b c-a d}{c(c x+d)}=\\frac{\\Delta}{x-\\frac{-d}{c}}</span></p><p>where</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\Delta=\\frac{b c-a d}{c^{2}}</span></p><p>If <span class=\"tex\">\\Delta=0</span>, then the graph of <span class=\"tex\">y</span> consists of the points <span class=\"tex\">\\left(x, \\frac{a}{c}\\right)</span>, except that the point <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\frac{-d}{c}, \\frac{a}{c}\\right)</span> is missing because the formula reads <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{0}{0}</span> when <span class=\"tex\">x=-\\frac{d}{c}</span>. In any case, this set of points is not symmetric with respect to the line <span class=\"tex\">y=x</span>. From now on, assume that <span class=\"tex\">\\Delta \\neq 0</span>. Multiply both sides by <span class=\"tex\">x-\\frac{-d}{c}</span> to find that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(x-\\frac{-d}{c}\\right)\\left(y-\\frac{a}{c}\\right)=\\Delta</span></p><p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">x-\\frac{-d}{c} \\neq 0</span> because <span class=\"tex\">\\Delta \\neq 0</span>. Thus, if it were desired, both sides of the new equation could be divided by <span class=\"tex\">x-\\frac{-c}{d}</span> to recover the former equation. Hence the set of pairs <span class=\"tex\">(x, y)</span> that satisfy the former equation is exactly the same as the set of <span class=\"tex\">(x, y)</span> that satisfy the new equation, which determines a rectangular hyperbola. This hyperbola is symmetric with respect to the line <span class=\"tex\">y=x</span> if and only if its center <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\frac{a}{c},-\\frac{d}{c}\\right)</span> lies on this line of symmetry. That is, <span class=\"tex\">d=-a</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">\\Delta \\neq 0</span> if and only if <span class=\"tex\">b c+a^{2} \\neq 0</span>.</p>\n<p>If <span class=\"tex\">a=0</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">b</span> and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> are free to take on any nonzero values, which gives <span class=\"tex\">10 \\cdot 10=100</span> quadruples <span class=\"tex\">(0, b, c, 0)</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">a \\neq 0</span>, then because <span class=\"tex\">d=-a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">c \\neq 0</span>, there are <span class=\"tex\">10 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 10=1100</span> quadruples <span class=\"tex\">(a, b, c,-a)</span> to consider. Note that <span class=\"tex\">b c+a^{2}=0</span> if and only if either <span class=\"tex\">|a|=|b|=|c|</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> and <span class=\"tex\">c</span> have opposite signs, which is <span class=\"tex\">10 \\cdot 2=20</span> cases, or <span class=\"tex\">|a|=2</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(b, c)=( \\pm 4, \\mp 1)</span> or <span class=\"tex\">( \\pm 1, \\mp 4)</span>, which is <span class=\"tex\">2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2=8</span> additional cases. Thus the total number of possibilities in Case 2 is <span class=\"tex\">100+1100-(20+8)=</span> 1172.</p>\n<p>In all there are <span class=\"tex\">120+1172=(\\text{B})\\boxed{1292}</span> quadruples satisfying the given conditions.</p>\n<p>Note: Functions of the form <span class=\"tex\">y=\\frac{a x+b}{c x+d}</span> with <span class=\"tex\">a d-b c \\neq 0</span> are known as \"linear-fractional\" or \"Möbius\". They arise in complex analysis and in number theory.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc12/2024/part-a/problem-25"}]});
